A||||7||WisconsinGT||Heads!
Z||000000||WisconsinGT||07-30-99||09:16 PM||||I am going to have my mechanic remove the heads on  my 2.8. I am wondering if there is any point in porting and polishing them? I am going to put new headers on as well. (Thanks Chris at WestCoast Fiero.) Is anyone using the small headers he sells? If so, what are your thoughts? <p>Also, when I start the car (88GT) after sitting overnight, I get a cloud of white smoke. It only happens after sitting. My mechanic says it could be antifreeze. Is this so?<p>Thanks 
Z||000001||JetroGT||07-30-99||10:15 PM||||My 86 SE did that untill I had the heads replaced. I had an extra set laying around, so I went to the machine shop and had them do a valve job on them $200. Had another shop swap them $900. After that no smoke on start up. The oil was leaking past the worn valves. But now I have a noticeable increce in HP.
Z||000002||JetroGT||07-30-99||10:16 PM||||My 86 SE did that untill I had the heads replaced. I had an extra set laying around, so I went to the machine shop and had them do a valve job on them $200. Had another shop swap them $900. After that no smoke on start up. The oil was leaking past the worn valves. But now I have a noticeable increce in HP.
Z||000003||batboy||07-30-99||10:17 PM||||Careful pocket porting of the heads will increase flow and power in most cases. Polishing is a waste of time and money, in my opinion. If the smoke is really white, that usually means a coolant leak such as a blown head gasket or cracked head. If the smoke has a blue tint to it, that usually means burning oil. Either way, do a compression check. A blown head gasket will sometimes cause two adjacent cylinders to be low on compression. The other telling tale is whether you are losing any coolant from the radiator or not.
Z||000004||Patrick||07-30-99||11:06 PM||||<br>White smoke is water vapor. If your coolant level is NOT going down, it's just condensation in your exhaust system evaporating. This is normal.<p>
Z||000005||lowCG||07-31-99||12:46 AM||||Just the textbook smoothing,and pocket porting that Batboy mentioned,I usually get the surfaces pretty shiny,just to ensure a smooth passage,then go back over with 180 grit paper to get rid of any shiny stuff,as the fuel will condense on this type of suface(not good),but I polish the heck out of the area 3/8" away from the valve seat,and right up to the seat area.<br>The whole ordeal shouldn't amount to much grinding really,remove the obvious casting imperfections/mismatching of cores within the intake ports,rarely waste any time on the exhaust side;that's under pressure by nature.
Z||000006||Bruce||08-11-99||08:19 PM||||lowCG,<br>I'm going to buy a pair of upgraded manifolds (V6) from Ed Parks. However, he doesn't do the porting and polishing there (work backlog).<br>How did your set turn out? I don't have any mechanical bent, so I'ld have to get them done at a machine shop. Any advice or suggestions?<br>thanks.
Z||000007||lowCG||08-12-99||01:55 AM||||Uh,mine are stuck to my motor,yet to be removed(getting big 4cam V6)and might stay there,but if I were to fix mine,I'd try not to go over-board on the grinding after the patching/welding,just remove any areas where the weld is perpendicular to the exhaust flow,on the port(engine)side of the weld.<br>If the "upgraded" manifolds are welded,and cleaned up after,they should work fine the way they come.<br>I'd go to a shop that specializes in welding things rather than an automotive machine shop,more likely to have an appreciation for a correctly fitting exhaust part,they'd probably grind'em out for a decent price as well,if you told them what you wanted/what you were doing.
